@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node MAIN
@next START

@{c}@{h1}NextWiki

@{c}English Wikipedia on the ZX Spectrum Next

Type a topic, press ENTER, and read the Wikipedia article on your Next.
Links to other articles are highlighted and can be followed. A NextWiki
server on the internet fetches the article from en.wikipedia.org, removes
everything except the article itself, and sends it a page at a time.

 @{" Starting NextWiki " LINK START}
 @{" The screen " LINK SCREEN}
 @{" Keys when reading an article " LINK KEYS_PAGE}
 @{" Keys in the Topic field " LINK KEYS_TOPIC}
 @{" Typing symbols " LINK SYMBOLS}
 @{" Finding articles " LINK FINDING}
 @{" Links and long articles " LINK LINKS}
 @{" Pictures " LINK PICTURES}
 @{" Credits and licences (A key) " LINK CREDITS}
 @{" Settings: NEXTWIKI.CFG " LINK CONFIG}
 @{" Messages on the status line " LINK MESSAGES}
 @{" What NextWiki cannot do " LINK LIMITS}
 @{" Quick key reference " LINK QUICKREF}

@{h2}Reading this guide

@{i}Right@{ui}/@{i}Left@{ui} select a link, @{i}ENTER@{ui} follows it, @{i}B@{ui} goes back.
@{i}N@{ui} and @{i}P@{ui} turn the pages, @{i}C@{ui} returns to the contents, @{i}Q@{ui} quits.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node START
@next SCREEN
@prev MAIN
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}Starting NextWiki

@{h2}Before the first use

1) The Next's Wi-Fi module must have joined your network once. Use the
   NextZXOS Wi-Fi setup program. The module remembers the network.
2) NextWiki.nex and NEXTWIKI.CFG must be in the same folder on the SD
   card, and NEXTWIKI.CFG must name your NextWiki server
   (see @{" Settings " LINK CONFIG}).

@{h2}Running it

Open the NextZXOS @{b}Browser@{ub}, go to the folder, select @{b}NextWiki.nex@{ub}
and press @{i}ENTER@{ui}. From NextBASIC you can also type:

    @{b}.nexload nextwiki.nex@{ub}

The welcome screen shows the server (only its first 5 letters; the rest
appear as *) and whether NEXTWIKI.CFG was read.

The Topic field already holds your home topic. Press @{i}ENTER@{ui} to read
it, or type another topic first, for example @{b}Albert Einstein@{ub}. The
first article takes a little longer because NextWiki checks the Wi-Fi
module.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node SCREEN
@next KEYS_PAGE
@prev START
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}The screen

From top to bottom:

 @{b}Topic bar@{ub}     " Topic: " then the field. Type a topic and press
               ENTER. On the right, the [ ] Images box: press I to
               tick it (see Pictures).
 @{b}Title bar@{ub}     The article title. "Page 2/7" on the right means you
               are reading page 2 of a 7-page article.
 @{b}Article@{ub}       Headings in yellow, text in white, links in purple.
               The selected link is shown in reverse.
               @{b}[ Next page ]@{ub} appears after the last line when the
               article continues.
 @{b}Help line@{ub}     The keys you can use right now.
 @{b}Status line@{ub}   What NextWiki is doing, or what went wrong (errors
               appear in red). When a link is selected it shows the
               article it leads to.

One thing has @{i}focus@{ui} at a time: the Topic field or the article. The
focused one is highlighted, and the help line changes with it.
@{i}T@{ui} jumps to the Topic field; @{i}ENTER@{ui} or a cursor key goes back.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node KEYS_PAGE
@next KEYS_TOPIC
@prev SCREEN
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}Keys when reading an article

@{i}Key                 What it does@{ui}

Cursor Down/Right   Choose the next link
Cursor Up/Left      Choose the previous link
ENTER               Open the chosen article (or [ Next page ])
SPACE               Scroll down one screen
DELETE              Scroll up one screen
N                   Next page of this article
B                   Back to where you were before
R                   Reload
H                   Home topic
T  (or U)           Type a new topic
I                   Pictures on/off
A                   Credits: authors and licences of this article
                    and its pictures (B returns)
Q                   Quit (then Y to confirm)

Links are chosen in reading order; if the next link is a long way down,
the cursor key scrolls the text instead so you never skip over anything.

These keys work the same way as in this guide viewer.

@{h2}Extra keys

EXTEND MODE        Switch between the article and the Topic field
EDIT               Un-choose the highlighted link
INV/TRUE VIDEO     Also scroll down / up one screen
@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node KEYS_TOPIC
@next SYMBOLS
@prev KEYS_PAGE
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}Keys in the Topic field

@{i}Key                 What it does@{ui}

letters, numbers    Type (hold CAPS SHIFT for capitals - not needed,
                    "albert einstein" works too)
SPACE               A space, as in "Albert Einstein"
SYMBOL SHIFT + key  Type a symbol such as - ' ( )  - see @{" Symbols " LINK SYMBOLS}
Cursor Left/Right   Move the cursor
DELETE              Rub out the character before the cursor
GRAPH               Clear the whole field
ENTER               Look the topic up
EDIT                Cancel: put back the article's title and return
                    to the article
Cursor Up/Down      Go back to the article

You never need to type a web address. (If you do type one, only English
Wikipedia article addresses are accepted.)
@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node SYMBOLS
@next FINDING
@prev KEYS_TOPIC
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}Typing symbols

Hold SYMBOL SHIFT and press the key:

@{i}Symbol  Key        Symbol  Key        Symbol  Key@{ui}
  -      J            '      7            (  )   8  9
  :      Z            /      V            .      M
  ,      N            &      6            !      1
  ?      C            +      K            =      L
  ;      O            "      P            _      0
  %      5            #      3            ~      A
  @@      2            *      B            $      4
  [      Y            ]      U            <  >   R  T
  |      S            \      D            {  }   F  G
  ^      H            `      X

Example: @{b}Mass-energy equivalence@{ub} needs SYMBOL SHIFT + J for the
hyphen.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node FINDING
@next LINKS
@prev SYMBOLS
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}Finding articles

Type the name of the thing you want to read about and press ENTER.

If Wikipedia has an article with exactly that name, it opens straight
away. Capital letters do not matter much: @{b}zx spectrum next@{ub} finds
@{b}ZX Spectrum Next@{ub}.

If there is no article with that exact name, NextWiki asks Wikipedia's
own search. When the search finds a clear match, that article opens.
Otherwise you get a page called @{b}Search: your words@{ub} listing the
matching articles with a short extract from each. Choose one with the
cursor keys and press ENTER.

Some titles have several meanings (Mercury: the planet, the element, the
god...). Wikipedia then shows a @{b}disambiguation page@{ub}; NextWiki says
so at the top. Choose the article you meant from its list.

If nothing at all is found, the status line says
@{b}No Wikipedia article found for "..."@{ub}. Check the spelling, or try
fewer words.

NextWiki only uses English Wikipedia. There is no language setting.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node LINKS
@next PICTURES
@prev FINDING
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}Links and long articles

@{h2}Following links

Press Down to select the first link, keep pressing to move through the
links, then press ENTER. The new article opens at its first page, and B
brings the old one back where you left it. Only links to other English
Wikipedia articles are shown as links.

@{h2}Long articles

The server cuts articles into pages of about 2000 characters, so the
beginning appears quickly. "Page 1/12" in the title bar means there are
12 pages. To read on, press @{b}N@{ub}, or select @{b}[ Next page ]@{ub} at the
end of the text and press ENTER. The last page has no [ Next page ].

Within a page, SPACE scrolls down a screen and DELETE scrolls back up.

The server downloads the article from Wikipedia once; turning the pages
only fetches the next part from the server.

@{h2}What is left out

Only the article itself is shown: no Wikipedia menus, no reference lists,
notes, further reading or external links, and no [1] citation marks. The
"Key facts" box (the infobox) is shown after the opening paragraphs.

@{h2}Attribution

The last page of every article ends with:

  Content from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia. Article contributors are
  credited under the applicable Creative Commons licence. See the original
  article on Wikipedia for attribution and licensing information.

followed by the address of the original article.

@{h2}History

B remembers the last 6 articles, including which page you were reading.

@{h2}Expired articles

The server keeps articles for a while (up to an hour). If you come back
after that, NextWiki fetches the article again by itself and carries on.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node PICTURES
@next CREDITS
@prev LINKS
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}Pictures

NextWiki can show up to three small pictures from each article: the first
three real pictures in the article, in order, that are freely licensed.
The server reads each picture's licence on Wikipedia and only uses
pictures that are public domain or under a free licence such as Creative
Commons BY or BY-SA. Copyrighted "fair use" pictures (logos, covers,
screenshots) are left out, so some articles have fewer pictures or none.
Icons, flags, maps and pictures from the reference sections are never
used. Pictures are off at first, so articles load as fast as possible.

Press @{b}I@{ub} in an article to tick the box at the top right:

    @{b}[X] Images@{ub}

The text appears first, as usual, with a line such as
@{b}[Image] Einstein in 1947@{ub} where each picture goes. Then the pictures
arrive one by one and the text moves down to make room for them. You can
keep reading and pressing keys while they load.

Press @{b}I@{ub} again to hide the pictures.

@{h2}What to expect

Pictures are small (at most about 160 by 120 dots) and use 16 colours, so
they are a rough preview. A page of an article shows at most 2 pictures;
a third one appears on a later page.

If a picture cannot be fetched you see @{b}[Image not available]@{ub} and its
caption instead. The text is not affected.

To have the box ticked every time NextWiki starts, put @{b}images=1@{ub} in
NEXTWIKI.CFG.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node CREDITS
@next CONFIG
@prev PICTURES
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}Credits and licences (A key)

Wikipedia's articles and pictures are written and made by many people
and may be reused only under their licences. Press @{b}A@{ub} while reading an
article to see its credits page:

 @{b}Source@{ub}        English Wikipedia, the article's title and address.
 @{b}Authors@{ub}       Who wrote it: the address of the article's history,
                where every contributor is listed.
 @{b}Text licence@{ub}  The licence of the text (Creative Commons Attribution-
                ShareAlike) and where to read its terms, and what
                NextWiki changed to fit the text on the screen.
 @{b}Pictures@{ub}      For each picture NextWiki can show: the file, its
                creator, source, date, licence, licence terms and any
                credit line the licence asks for.

Each picture's licence is read separately from its own page on
Wikipedia; pictures do not all have the same licence, and pictures whose
licence is not free, or cannot be found, are never shown.

Press @{b}B@{ub} to go back to the article where you were.

The last page of every article also ends with a short notice naming
Wikipedia as the source and giving the original article's address.

NextWiki itself is separate software with its own licence (NOTICE.TXT,
next to NextWiki.nex). NextWiki only shows Wikipedia content while you
read; it does not save, collect or pass on articles or pictures.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node CONFIG
@next MESSAGES
@prev CREDITS
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}Settings: NEXTWIKI.CFG

NEXTWIKI.CFG is a text file in the same folder as NextWiki.nex. Each line
is @{b}name=value@{ub}; lines starting with # are ignored. Any line can be left
out to use the built-in default.

@{i}Setting          Meaning@{ui}
server=          Your NextWiki server's name, for example
                 myserver.azurewebsites.net (host:port also works)
port=            Server port, normally 80
key=             Access key, if your server needs one
home=            The topic shown at start-up and opened by H
                 (default ZX Spectrum Next)
text_colour=     Colour of article text       (default white)
link_colour=     Colour of links              (default purple)
screen_rows=     Rows used on screen, 22 to 32 (default 24)
images=          1 = pictures on at start-up   (default 0)
tls=             Leave at 0 (experimental)

@{h2}Colours

Use a name: white black grey lightgrey red green blue yellow cyan magenta
purple orange pink lightblue lightgreen brown. Or give a Next colour number
(RRRGGGBB) such as 171, 0xAB, $AB or #AB.

@{h2}screen_rows

24 uses the normal Spectrum screen area, which every TV and monitor shows.
If your display shows more of the border, try 26, 28 or even 32 to see more
text at once. If the top or bottom gets cut off, use a lower number.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node MESSAGES
@next LIMITS
@prev CONFIG
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}Messages on the status line

@{b}Type a topic, for example Albert Einstein@{ub}
  ENTER was pressed with an empty Topic field.
@{b}Error: No Wikipedia article found for "..."@{ub}
  Neither an article nor Wikipedia's search found anything. Check the
  spelling or use fewer words.
@{b}Error: No Wikipedia article called "..."@{ub}
  A link led to an article that no longer exists.
@{b}Error: Only English Wikipedia articles can be opened@{ub}
  You typed the address of another website or another language.
@{b}Error: Wikipedia is busy (HTTP 429) - try again shortly@{ub}
  Wikipedia asked NextWiki to slow down. Wait a moment and press R.
@{b}Error: Site returned HTTP 503@{ub} (or another number)
  Wikipedia reported a problem. Press R to try again later.
@{b}Error: Access key missing or wrong@{ub}
  key= in NEXTWIKI.CFG does not match the server.
@{b}Wi-Fi module not responding@{ub}
  The Wi-Fi module did not answer. Check it is fitted; try .uart and AT.
@{b}Wi-Fi not connected - run the NextZXOS Wi-Fi setup@{ub}
  The module has not joined a network. Run the Wi-Fi setup once.
@{b}Connection failed (server unreachable or DNS)@{ub}
  Check server= and port= in NEXTWIKI.CFG, and that the server is running.
@{b}Connection failed: Connection timed out@{ub}
  Weak Wi-Fi, or the server was asleep. Press R to try again.
@{b}Page loaded, but the response was incomplete@{ub}
  The connection dropped. Press R.
@{b}Article expired on the server - reloading...@{ub}
  Normal after a long pause; NextWiki fetches the article again.
@{b}Image not available@{ub}
  A picture could not be fetched or is not a supported kind. Only that
  picture is missing; the text is fine.
@{b}Not enough free memory for images@{ub}
  NextZXOS had no spare memory for pictures. Text still works.

After any error the article you were reading stays on screen, and the
topic you typed is kept so you can correct it. B and R still work.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node LIMITS
@next QUICKREF
@prev MESSAGES
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}What NextWiki cannot do

@{b}English Wikipedia only.@{ub} Other languages and other websites cannot
be opened.

@{b}No bold or italics.@{ub} The Next's text screen has one style; words that
are bold or italic on Wikipedia look like normal text.

@{b}Tables are simplified.@{ub} Each row becomes one line with | between
the cells. Formulas appear as their TeX source, for example a^{2}+b^{2}.

@{b}Plain letters only.@{ub} Accents are removed (an accented e shows as a
plain e) and other alphabets, such as Japanese or Cyrillic, show as ?.

@{b}Not private.@{ub} The connection between the Next and the NextWiki server
is not encrypted, so anyone on the network path can see what you read.

@{b}No editing, logins or talk pages.@{ub} NextWiki is for reading articles.

@-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node QUICKREF
@prev LIMITS
@toc MAIN
@{r}NextWiki Guide
@{c}@{h1}Quick key reference

@{h2}Reading an article
 Cursor keys   choose a link        ENTER    open it
 SPACE         scroll down          DELETE   scroll up
 N   next page      B   back        R   reload
 H   home topic     T   new topic   Q   quit
 I   pictures on/off               A   credits and licences

@{h2}Typing a topic
 SYMBOL SHIFT + J -   7 '   8 (   9 )   N ,   Z :
 Cursor Left/Right   move     DELETE   rub out     GRAPH   clear
 ENTER   look up              EDIT     cancel
